WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.100
<v Instructor>Now let's talk about AWS managed logs.</v>

2
00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.110
So this is the kind of logs that can be produced

3
00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:06.351
by the AWS services that can be helpful

4
00:00:06.351 --> 00:00:08.490
for your solution architecture.

5
00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:10.800
The first one is the load balancer access logs

6
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.452
to get details of all the requests meant

7
00:00:13.452 --> 00:00:15.360
to your load balancers.

8
00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.030
And so it's for the ALB, the NLB and the CLB.

9
00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:20.730
And you can export these lugs into S3.

10
00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:22.440
CloudTrail Logs which are the logs

11
00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:24.750
of all the API calls met within your accounts,

12
00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.870
and this can be exported to S3 and CloudWatch Logs.

13
00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:30.360
VPC Flow Logs, which is going to give you information

14
00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:33.390
about all the IP traffic going to and from

15
00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:35.670
your network interfaces, in your VPC.

16
00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:38.130
And this can be sent to S3, to CloudWatch Logs

17
00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:40.260
and to Kinesis Data Firehose.

18
00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:43.650
You have Route 53 Access Logs and these can be sent

19
00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.630
and streamed directly into CloudWatch Logs

20
00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:47.820
and they will log information about all the queries

21
00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.580
that Route 53 is receiving.

22
00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:51.690
S3 Access Logs where

23
00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:53.790
you can send this to another S3 bucket

24
00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:55.290
which is giving you some information

25
00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:57.810
about all these access logins that happens

26
00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:00.900
for the request made within your bucket.

27
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.090
CloudFront Access Logs to S3

28
00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:04.710
which is going to give you detailed information

29
00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:07.650
about every user request that CloudFront will receive.

30
00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:11.700
And finally, all the config information can be exported

31
00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:15.360
into S3, so you can get all your configuration exported

32
00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.180
as a backup and do some analysis on them.

33
00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.620
Okay, so that's it for all you need to know

34
00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:21.720
just a very small informational lecture.

35
00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:24.623
I hope you liked it and I will see you in the next lecture.

